# sci-etl-core

A reusable, **domain-agnostic** Python library for scientific text mining and
ETL. `sci-etl-core` gives you composable building blocks — extractors, parsers,
LLM clients, processors, exporters, and state managers — behind clean abstract
base classes, so you can assemble a pipeline for *any* corpus without inheriting
constants tied to a specific field of science.

The library ships an **async core with a generated synchronous facade**: every
implementation is written once as `async` code, and the blocking API is
generated from it. You get first-class `asyncio` performance and an ergonomic
sync surface from the same source of truth.

---

## Features

- **Six pluggable layers** behind abstract base classes: `Extractor`, `Parser`,
  `LLMClient` (plus `EntityExtractor` / `RelevanceFilter`), `Processor`,
  `Exporter`, `StateManager`.
- **Async-first, sync-friendly** — write once as `async`, call it either way.
- **Dependency injection everywhere** — no hard-coded sources, models, or
  destinations. Swap any collaborator at construction time.
- **Built-in orchestration** via `ETLPipeline` / `AsyncETLPipeline` and a
  `ProcessorChain` for staged dataframe transforms.
- **Pluggable rate limiting** — `SemaphoreRateLimiter`, token-bucket
  `AioLimiterRateLimiter`, or `NullRateLimiter`, selected by `build_rate_limiter`.
- **Typed configuration** from YAML + `.env` with Pydantic validation and
  `SecretStr` secret handling.
- **Concrete implementations included**: arXiv extractor; OpenAI-compatible LLM
  client; PDF / LaTeX / HTML parsers; CSV upsert, SQL table, and 3D Plotly
  exporters; file-based state manager.
- **Offline-first test suite** — pytest with mocks, Hypothesis property tests,
  and ABC conformance contract tests.
- **PEP 561 typed** (`py.typed`) for downstream type checking.

## Installation

```bash
pip install sci-etl-core
```

Optional extras pull in only what a given workload needs:

```bash
pip install "sci-etl-core[async]"   # httpx, aiofiles, aiolimiter
pip install "sci-etl-core[llm]"     # OpenAI-compatible client + tiktoken
pip install "sci-etl-core[sql]"     # SQLAlchemy async engine
pip install "sci-etl-core[viz]"     # plotly, pandas
```

## Quick Start

### Async pipeline (canonical)

```python
import asyncio

from sci_etl_core import (
    AsyncArxivExtractor,
    AsyncOpenAICompatibleClient,
    AsyncLLMEntityExtractor,
    AsyncLLMRelevanceFilter,
    AsyncCsvUpsertExporter,
    AsyncFileStateManager,
    AsyncETLPipeline,
)
from sci_etl_core.http_async import build_async_client
from sci_etl_core.parsers import PdfPlumberParser, LatexTarballParser
from sci_etl_core.processors import DefaultKeyNormalizer


async def main() -> None:
    client = build_async_client()
    llm = AsyncOpenAICompatibleClient(
        api_key="sk-...",
        base_url="https://api.openai.com/v1",
        model="gpt-4o-mini",
    )

    pipeline = AsyncETLPipeline(
        extractor=AsyncArxivExtractor(
            client=client,
            pdf_parser=PdfPlumberParser(),
            latex_parser=LatexTarballParser(),
        ),
        relevance_filter=AsyncLLMRelevanceFilter(
            llm_client=llm,
            system_prompt="Return {'relevant': bool} for the given abstract.",
        ),
        entity_extractor=AsyncLLMEntityExtractor(
            llm_client=llm,
            system_prompt="Extract measurements as {'items': [...]}.",
        ),
        exporter=AsyncCsvUpsertExporter(
            key_column="record_id",
            value_columns=["value_a", "value_b"],
            normalizer=DefaultKeyNormalizer(),
        ),
        state_manager=AsyncFileStateManager("processed.txt", "state.json"),
        destination="results.csv",
        closeables=[client],
    )

    async with pipeline:
        processed = await pipeline.run(query="all:galaxy", total_limit=50)
    print(f"Processed {processed} records")


asyncio.run(main())
```

### Sync usage (generated facade)

The synchronous classes mirror the async ones one-to-one; they wrap the async
implementation and block until it completes.

```python
from sci_etl_core import (
    ArxivExtractor,
    OpenAICompatibleClient,
    LLMEntityExtractor,
    CsvUpsertExporter,
)
from sci_etl_core.http_async import build_async_client
from sci_etl_core.parsers import PdfPlumberParser, LatexTarballParser
from sci_etl_core.processors import DefaultKeyNormalizer

extractor = ArxivExtractor(
    client=build_async_client(),
    pdf_parser=PdfPlumberParser(),
    latex_parser=LatexTarballParser(),
)
listing = extractor.search("all:galaxy", max_results=25, start_index=0)
records, total = extractor.parse_listing(listing, seen_ids=set())

llm = OpenAICompatibleClient(
    api_key="sk-...",
    base_url="https://api.openai.com/v1",
    model="gpt-4o-mini",
)
entities = LLMEntityExtractor(
    llm_client=llm,
    system_prompt="Extract measurements as {'items': [...]}.",
).extract(extractor.fetch_full_text(records[0]))

CsvUpsertExporter(
    key_column="record_id",
    value_columns=["value_a", "value_b"],
    normalizer=DefaultKeyNormalizer(),
).export(entities, "results.csv")
```

> **Event-loop note.** The sync facade drives the async core on a private
> background event loop. Call the sync API from ordinary blocking code, **not**
> from inside a running `asyncio` loop — in async contexts, use the `Async*`
> classes directly.

### Configuration

```python
from pathlib import Path
from sci_etl_core.config import BaseAppConfig, load_config

config = load_config(BaseAppConfig, Path("config.yaml"), Path(".env"))
print(config.llm.model, config.pipeline.max_records)
```

```yaml
# config.yaml
llm:
  base_url: https://api.openai.com/v1
  model: gpt-4o-mini
http:
  max_retries: 3
pipeline:
  search_query: "all:galaxy"
  max_records: 100
```

The API key is read from the environment (`LLM_API_KEY` by default) and stored
as a Pydantic `SecretStr`, so it never appears in logs or reprs.

## Architecture

```
                         +----------------------+
                         |     ETLPipeline      |  sync facade
                         |   AsyncETLPipeline   |  async core
                         +----------+-----------+
                                    |
      +---------------+-------------+-------------+----------------+
      v               v             v             v                v
+-----------+  +-------------+  +---------+  +-----------+  +--------------+
| Extractor |  |  Relevance  |  | Entity  |  | Exporter  |  |    State     |
|           |  |   Filter    |  |Extractor|  |           |  |   Manager    |
+-----+-----+  +------+------+  +----+----+  +-----+-----+  +------+-------+
      |               |             |              |               |
      v               v             v              v               v
  Parser(s)       LLMClient     LLMClient      CSV / SQL /      processed ids
  PDF/LaTeX/HTML  (OpenAI-      (OpenAI-       Plotly 3D        + run metadata
                   compatible)   compatible)

              Processors: ProcessorChain -> Normalize -> Dedup
                          -> Cluster -> Quality -> Validate

  Cross-cutting: config (YAML + .env, Pydantic) | logging (file + console)
                 rate limiting (Null / Semaphore / AioLimiter)
```

Every box is an abstract base class with at least one concrete implementation.
The async core is the single source of truth; the sync classes are generated
from it and bridged through a background event loop.

## Results Showcase

A typical run over a corpus produces a normalized, deduplicated CSV plus an
interactive 3D scatter plot:

| record_id   | value_a | value_b | quality |
|-------------|---------|---------|---------|
| 2401.01234  | 12.4    | 0.87    | ok      |
| 2402.05678  | 9.1     | 0.64    | ok      |
| 2403.09012  | 15.8    | 0.92    | flagged |

```python
from sci_etl_core import Plotly3DExporter, ScatterPlotConfig

Plotly3DExporter(
    ScatterPlotConfig(
        x_column="value_a",
        y_column="value_b",
        z_column="quality_score",
        color_column="cluster",
        title="Corpus overview",
    )
).export(dataframe, "overview.html")
```

## Testing

```bash
pip install -e ".[async,llm,sql,viz]"
pip install pytest pytest-asyncio pytest-mock hypothesis
pytest                     # full suite
pytest --cov=sci_etl_core  # with coverage
```

The suite is **offline-first**: network, LLM, and filesystem collaborators are
mocked, so tests run deterministically with no external services. Property tests
(Hypothesis) and ABC conformance contract tests guard the public interfaces.

